  "review": {
    "note": "Candidate Hindi (Devanagari) and Hinglish (romanized) crisis-lexicon terms, written by the dev team as a starting point for the native-speaker and safe-messaging review (PRD 21 A9, REQ-SAFE-03, REQ-SAFE-15). Nothing here is read by the app or the server: the active, signed lexicon is contracts/safety/crisis_lexicon.v1.json, whose hi / hi-Latn languages stay `placeholder` and whose UI stays hidden (AURA_BETA_LOCALES) until reviewed terms move there.",
    "how_to_adopt": [
      "A native Hindi reader reviews every entry and variant (keep, change, drop, add), especially spellings people actually type.",
      "AK checks each entry against the WHO / AFSP / Samaritans safe-messaging checklist (A9).",
      "Move the kept entries into crisis_lexicon.v1.json, set hi / hi-Latn to `alpha`, bump `version`, add the kept cases below to crisis_lexicon.vectors.json, run `python3 apps/mobile/tool/gen_lexicon.py`, and re-sign the fixture (contracts/safety/README.md 'Changing the lexicon').",
      "Build a 200-phrase recall set per language (REQ-SAFE-03) before un-hiding Hindi / Hinglish."
    ],
    "open": [
      "Devanagari forms match as substrings, so 'मरना चाह' also catches 'मरना चाहिए' (should die): intended (a gentle sheet beats a miss), but review.",
      "'मर गए यार' / 'mar gaye yaar' is usually hyperbole; kept `soft` so it never opens the crisis sheet. Confirm with the reviewer.",
      "'khud ko mar d' is a stem for 'khud ko mar dunga / mar dalunga / mar du'; check it does not over-match 'khud ko mar dekh' style phrasing.",
      "'nas kaat' (cutting veins) names a method: detection only, it is never shown to anyone (REQ-SAFE-15 concerns shown copy).",
      "No gendered verb forms are needed in Devanagari (stems stop before the gendered ending); Hinglish uses `stem` for the same reason."
    ]
  },
